  "image_name": "c4acc339573831963d9268470e4e7bf5.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure is a conceptual overview of the phosphorus cycle linking land, humans, fresh water, and the ocean. On land, phosphorus originates from rock phosphate deposits, mining, weathering, volcanic inputs, and dust. Human activities then redirect it through fertilizer production and manufacturing, with phosphorus moving into agriculture, soils, livestock manure, and human consumption. From there, it reaches rivers and lakes through both diffuse runoff from land and point discharges such as sewage treatment.\n\nThe diagram emphasizes how multiple terrestrial pathways converge on fresh water and then the marine environment. Natural erosion and atmospheric dust also deliver material directly toward aquatic systems. Once phosphorus enters the sea, it is incorporated into biomass and then recycled through biological processes, including grazing and viral lysis. Some of this particulate phosphorus aggregates and sinks, where it is remineralized or buried through sedimentation and diagenesis. Upwelling returns some regenerated phosphorus from deeper waters back toward surface biomass, showing a loop between biological uptake and ocean recycling.\n\nOverall, the image highlights the strong coupling between geological sources, human management of phosphorus, transport through watersheds, and microbial or ecological recycling in the ocean.",
